  predicate for "should fire" is:

  !task.done && task.dueAt !== [34mnull[39m && task.dueAt <= now && task.notifiedAt === [34mnull[39m

  Startup and visibility ticks are what make this robust to the tab sleeping or the laptop suspending — the check
  compares timestamps rather than trusting that an interval kept running, so a machine that was asleep for six hours
  catches up correctly the moment it wakes.

  [1mRe-arming on edit.[0m If you push a due date into the future, [38;5;153mapplyChange[39m resets [38;5;153mnotifiedAt[39m to [38;5;153mnull[39m. Otherwise a
  rescheduled task would stay permanently silent — a bug that would be genuinely baffling to diagnose later, so the rule
  belongs in the chokepoint where it can't be forgotten.

  [1mCompleting suppresses.[0m Handled free by the [38;5;153m!done[39m clause. Finish a task before it's due and nothing fires.

  [1mThe overdue-backlog problem, which I want your call on.[0m Open the app after a week away with twelve overdue tasks and
  the naive implementation fires twelve browser notifications at once. That's not a reminder system, that's an attack.
  Options:

  - [1mCollapse above a threshold[0m — fire individually up to 3, otherwise one summary: [3m"12 tasks are overdue."[0m All twelve
    still get marked notified. My preference: it degrades gracefully and the summary is more useful than twelve separate
    popups anyway.
  - [1mSuppress stale ones[0m — don't fire for anything more than a day overdue, just show it in the UI. Quieter, but it
    silently drops things you might have wanted to see.
  - [1mFire everything[0m — simplest, honest, occasionally terrible.

  [1mNotification permission, which is a UX trap worth avoiding.[0m Requesting [38;5;153mNotification.permission[39m on page load is the
  pattern every site uses and every user hates, and Chrome and Firefox now actively penalize it — ask at a bad moment
  and you can get permanently blocked with no recourse. Instead: never prompt on load; prompt the first time you set a
  due date on a task, which is the moment the request actually makes sense. If permission is denied or unavailable, fall
  back to an in-app banner and never ask again. The app stays fully functional with notifications denied — browser
  notifications are an enhancement, not the mechanism.

  The threshold question is the one I need from you; my recommendation is collapse-above-3.

